Earth System Models (ESMs) are comprehensive tools designed to simulate the interconnected processes of the Earth's atmosphere, land, rivers, and oceans (Golaz et al., 2019, 2022) (Figure 1a). While ESMs have advanced in coupling riverine and coastal processes (Feng et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2022, 2024), they face critical challenges in accurately representing CF dynamics at the river-ocean interface (Ward et al., 2020). In particular, the current ESM framework employs one-dimensional (1D) large-scale river models and two-dimensional (2D) or three-dimensional (3D) ocean models (Figure 1b), each optimized for their respective domains. Specifically, the 1D large-scale river models, which are computationally efficient and suitable for channelized flow where pressure gradients dominate, employ reduced physics governing equations, such as kinematic wave or diffusion wave approximations of full dynamic Saint Venant equations (SVE) (Feng, Tan et al., 2022; Ikeuchi et al., 2017). These simplifications cannot accurately represent inertial momentum in low-lying regions (Hodges, 2013). Additionally, despite variable-resolution capabilities, these models cannot achieve the spatial resolution required to resolve fine-scale processes within river channels due to inherent large-scale modeling assumptions (Li et al., 2013). Conversely, ocean models, though capable of resolving detailed coastal dynamics with high-resolution meshes in estuaries, face their own challenges. Extending the computational domain of an ocean model upstream into river networks substantially increases computational demands through additional grid cells and smaller time steps (Lilly et al., 2023). Moreover, applying 2D/3D models to predominantly 1D channelized flow represents an inefficient use of computational resources (Hoch et al., 2017), and may introduce unnecessary complexity in regions where flow is primarily governed by along-channel momentum.

Over the past decade, machine learning (ML) has emerged as a promising solution for addressing the computational and physical modeling challenges in flood prediction (Kratzert et al., 2018; Nearing et al., 2024; Tran et al., 2023). In the context of ESMs, ML provides a pathway to incorporate subgrid-scale processes into large-scale simulations, enabling efficient representations of flood dynamics across multiple spatial and temporal scales (Reichstein et al., 2019; Willard et al., 2022). ML models can serve as computationally efficient alternatives that capture fine-scale flood dynamics while maintaining numerical stability and physical consistency (Sit et al., 2020). By integrating into existing ESM frameworks (Camps-Valls et al., 2021), ML models can serve as flexible intermediate-scale models that improve the representation of river-ocean coupling, which is critical for simulating extreme events.

ML applications have demonstrated success across various flooding scenarios, including river flooding (Jiang et al., 2022; Nevo et al., 2022), coastal flooding (Dey et al., 2024; Hasan et al., 2023), urban flooding (Hou

