et al., 2021; Lei et al., 2021), compound flooding (Di Bacco et al., 2024; Munoz et al., 2021), and flash flooding (Khosravi et al., 2018; Ma et al., 2021). Broadly, ML approaches can be categorized into two paradigms: data-driven models that infer system behavior purely from observational or simulation data and physics-informed models that embed physical laws within learning algorithms. Each approach faces distinct trade-offs in terms of accuracy, generalizability, and computational demands.

Data-driven approaches have shown remarkable success in capturing complex spatial and temporal patterns in flood dynamics (Sit et al., 2020). They offer substantial improvements in predictive accuracy while reducing computational costs compared with traditional numerical methods. The evolution of data-driven models has progressed from simple statistical methods to sophisticated deep learning architectures, with each advancement offering new capabilities for handling the complexities of flood dynamics. Traditional ML methods, including Linear Regression (Tsalxiri et al., 2018), Radial Basis Function (Panigarishi et al., 2019), Support Vector Regression (Panahi et al., 2021), Decision Trees (Pham et al., 2021), and Random Forests (Chen et al., 2020), have established foundational frameworks for flood prediction. Recent advances in computing power and algorithm design have enabled more sophisticated approaches, such as Gaussian Process Regression (Betancourt et al., 2020) and gradient boosting methods such as XGBoost (Lee et al., 2024), which offer improved predictive capabilities while maintaining interpretability. Moreover, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) have proven particularly effective in spatial feature extraction and flood extent mapping (Wang et al., 2020) while Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) excel at capturing temporal dependencies in flow dynamics (Graves and Graves, 2012; Nearing et al., 2024; Xiang et al., 2020). These architectures have been successfully applied to various flood-related tasks, including water level or discharge predictions (Pan et al., 2020; Solanki et al., 2025), flood forecasting (Le et al., 2019), ID/2D flow simulations (Crompton et al., 2019; Maxwell et al., 2021; Pang, 2024; Zhang et al., 2024), and flood inundation modeling (Kabir et al., 2020). Recent studies have demonstrated the capability of data-driven models to simulate compound flooding in river systems by incorporating both upstream discharge and downstream water level data (Moradian et al., 2024). Data-driven models rely solely on historical data for training, potentially capturing complex phenomena that may be difficult to describe through physical equations alone (Yin et al., 2021). However, these approaches face challenges in extrapolating beyond their training data (Frame et al., 2022), particularly for extreme events such as peak streamflow conditions (Kim et al., 2021). This limitation necessitates careful consideration of training data selection and generation strategies (Fraehr et al., 2025).

Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) represent a novel method in physics-informed ML, combining the universal approximation capabilities of neural networks with physical constraints derived from governing equations (Raissi et al., 2019). In the context of flood modeling, PINNs offer a unique advantage by enforcing conservation laws and physical boundary and initial conditions directly within the neural network architecture, ensuring conservation of mass and momentum (Karniadakis et al., 2021). Several variants of PINNs have emerged to address specific computational challenges: numerical method based PINNs leverage structured grid discretization to improve gradient computation accuracy and training stability (Jiang et al., 2023; Lim et al., 2022; Xiang et al., 2022). Self-adaptive PINNs incorporating attention mechanisms have also been developed to better handle multi-scale physics and complex boundary conditions (Xiang et al., 2021). Recent applications of PINNs in water modeling have demonstrated promising results across various scenarios. These include groundwater flow (Cuomo et al., 2023) and surface water dynamics using 1D kinematic wave equations (Niu et al., 2023), diffusive wave equations (Hou et al., 2024), dynamic wave equations (Dazzi, 2024; Feng, Tan, & He, 2023; Luo et al., 2024), and 2D shallow water equations (Qi et al., 2024; Tian et al., 2025; Zhou et al., 2025). The ability of PINNs to handle complex boundary conditions and maintain physical consistency makes them particularly suitable for modeling CF at river-ocean interfaces. The key advantage of PINNs lies in their ability to incorporate physical knowledge directly into the learning process, potentially reducing the required training data while ensuring physically consistent solutions (Cai et al., 2021). In addition to learning the underlying hydrodynamics, PINN has the unique capability of assimilating observational data (He et al., 2020; He & Tartakovsky, 2021). However, challenges remain in terms of training stability, computational efficiency, and scalability to complex geometries (Cuomo et al., 2022).

Despite their different approaches, both PINNs and data-driven models offer complementary strengths for flood modeling. PINNs provide physically consistent solutions and require less training data but may be computationally intensive during training (Karniadakis et al., 2021). Data-driven models offer faster training and inference times and can capture complex patterns from data but require extensive training data sets (Willard et al., 2022).

They lack the ability to intrinsically incorporate physical laws, which may lead to non-physical predictions under extrapolative conditions (Brunton and Kutz, 2022). A comparison of these approaches in the context of CF modeling remains largely unexplored, particularly regarding their integration potential with ESMs (Jafarzadegan et al., 2023).

This study presents a systematic comparison of physics-informed and data-driven neural networks for CF modeling, with a particular focus on hurricane-induced CF during Hurricane Irene (2011). We use a small river reach (Lower Delaware River) extracted from a global ESM framework as a testbed to assess model performance and computational efficiency. To improve the efficiency of PINN, we devised an innovative architecture that uses the finite difference scheme to replace the computational expensive _automatic differentiation_ (AD) in vanilla PINNs. We also developed a new training data generation approach that effectively captures extreme fluvial and coastal floods. This approach ensures that the data set is well-suited for training data-driven models. By evaluating different ML architectures, including vanilla PINN, finite-difference-based (FD) PINN, and various data-driven models, we aim to identify optimal approaches for simulating CF at the river-ocean interface of ESMs.

## 2 Methods

This study evaluates the performance of physics-informed and data-driven machine learning models to enhance the representation of hydrodynamic processes in the 1D channel at the interface between river and ocean components in ESMs (Figure 1a). This 1D channel is extracted from the coarser-resolution river model mesh used in ESMs and refined to a higher resolution suitable for machine learning applications. At this interface, the river and ocean models provide the upstream and downstream boundary conditions, respectively (Figure 2a). For this refined 1D domain, we implement two distinct ML modeling strategies:

1. Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN): The PINN-based models are set up to take boundary condition (BC) information from ESMs, along with available observational or synthetic data, to provide a high-resolution hydrodynamic solution.
2. Data-Driven Neural Networks: data-driven models require a high-resolution numerical model to generate training data. A hydrodynamic model is set up to simulate historical flood events, producing a data set of spatiotemporal water depth variations under different boundary conditions. This data set is then used to train various data-driven models (CNN, U-Net, Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU)), which learn statistical relationships between input boundary conditions and flood dynamics.

By testing these models, we aim to assess their advantages and limitations in resolving the compounding hydrodynamic processes. The objective is to determine the most effective approach for integrating ML into ESMs to improve CF simulations at finer scales.

### Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINN)

Here, we first introduce the vanilla PINN model (hereafter, standard PINN) (Feng et al., 2023; Raissi et al., 2019) to solve the one-dimensional (1D) Saint-Venant equations (SVE), which describe unsteady, gradually varied open-channel flow. PINN is a mesh-free deep learning framework that embeds the SVE as soft constraints in the loss function, ensuring that the learned solutions satisfy physical laws. Unlike purely data-driven models, PINNs do not require pre-generated training data sets from numerical simulations. We apply several enhancements, including adaptive weighting (Wang et al., 2021), Fourier feature embedding (Wong et al., 2022), and \(L_{2}\) regularization (Wang et al., 2023), to improve the accuracy and stability of the PINN framework (Feng et al., 2023; Fan and He, 2023).

The 1D SVE consists of the continuity and momentum equations

\[\frac{\partial h}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial uh}{\partial x}=q_{s}, \tag{1}\]

\[\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}+u\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}+s\frac{\partial h }{\partial x}+g\big{(}S_{f}-S\big{)}=0, \tag{2}\]where \(t\) is time, \(x\) is the distance along the channel, \(h\) is water depth, \(u\) is flow velocity, \(g\) is gravity \(q_{s}\) is source inflow and is assumed as 0 in this study. \(S\) and \(S_{f}\) are the bottom slope and the friction slope, respectively, with the latter obtained using the Chezy-Manning equation:

\[S_{f}=\frac{n^{2}|u|u}{R^{\frac{2}{4}}}, \tag{3}\]

Figure 2: (a) The schematic of the PINN-based methods for solving the CF dynamics of the 1D channel at the river-ocean interface. The data from the downstream ocean and upstream river models are provided as BCs at the channel’s downstream (\(x=x_{b}\)) and upstream (\(x=x_{a}\)) boundaries, respectively, where \(n\) is the number of grid cells. The remote sensing data are imposed at a larger time interval than the simulation time step at \(t=t_{0}\) and \(t=t_{w}\), where \(n\) is the number of time steps. In situ time series data at the available sites within the channel domain are also assimilated. (b) The schematic of the standard PINN and FD-PINN framework, where the densely connected neural network (NN) takes the input features of the space variable \(x\) and the time variable \(t\) and output the velocity \(u\) and water depth \(h\). In this study, adaptive weighting is used to balance the loss terms, and \(t\) is transformed by Fourier feature embedding before feeding into the neural network. The vanilla PINN and FD-PINN use _automatic differentiation_ and finite difference method (FD), respectively, to calculate the derivatives with respect to \(x\) and \(t\). (c) The framework of the data-driven NN models. The training data are generated from numerical simulations using the method described in Section 2.3. The input features of the NN are the time series data at the ocean and river BCs and the output feature is the spatiotemporal distributed water depth. Once trained, the data-driven models predict water depth for an unseen flood event based on new boundary condition inputs.

where \(n\) is Manning's roughness coefficient and the hydraulic radius \(R\) takes the form: \(R\ =\ bh/(2h\ +\ b)\), where \(b\) is the channel width.

PINN approximates the solution to these equations using a deep neural network (DNN). The input layer consists of spatial (\(x\)) and temporal (\(t\)) coordinates while the output layer predicts the unknown state variables \(h(x,t,\theta)\) and \(u(x,t,\theta)\), where \(\theta\) is the vector of weights and biases. The governing equations are enforced as soft constraints using AD (Abadi et al., 2016), ensuring that the learned solution satisfies the underlying physics. The neural network architecture for PINN consists of a fully connected feed-forward DNN with \(L\) hidden layers, each containing \(N_{L}\) neurons.

In this study, the designed PINN network uses the \(\tanh\) activation function, which has been found to improve gradient propagation in physics-based learning problems. The network parameters (weights and biases) are optimized using the Adam optimizer with an exponentially decaying learning rate. The total loss function in our PINN framework is formulated as

\[\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathcal{L}_{\text{PDE}}(\theta)+\sum_{j}w_{j}\mathcal{L }_{j}(\theta)+L_{\text{reg}}, \tag{4}\]

where \(\mathcal{L}_{\text{PDE}}\) denotes the loss term of the governing PDE's residuals, \(\mathcal{L}_{j}\) represent loss contributions from boundary conditions and various data sources, including upstream and downstream boundaries (i.e., at \(x_{0}\) and \(x_{n}\)), remote sensing snapshots at the revisit time of satellites (i.e., \(t_{0}\) and \(t_{m}\)), and in situ time-series data at the available gauges (Figure 1(b)). \(\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}\) denotes the regularization term. Both gauged time series measurements and spatial snapshots from remote sensing can be incorporated into the PINN training via these additional loss terms. While PINNs can, in principle, be trained using only initial and boundary conditions, the high-resolution initial conditions are not available in the targeted domain of ESMs. Thus, the remote sensing data are used to constrain the spatial evolution of the solution.

The weights \(w_{j}\) are used to balance the contributions of each corresponding \(\mathcal{L}_{j}\). A critical challenge in training PINNs is to balance different loss terms as the scales of residuals from PDEs, boundary conditions, and data constraints can vary significantly. Fixed weighting schemes often lead to imbalanced issues, where one term dominates the loss function, causing poor convergence. To address this, we implement an adaptive weighting strategy based on the gradient balancing method (Wang et al., 2021). The weighting coefficients \(w_{j}\) are updated dynamically at each training step to balance the gradients of different loss terms. The value of \(w_{j}\) at the \(k\)-th step of gradient descent during the training is provided as

\[w_{j}=(1-\alpha)\,w_{j}+\alpha\,\frac{\max_{\theta}\lvert\nabla_{\theta} \mathcal{L}_{\text{PDE}}(\theta_{k})\rvert}{\lvert\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_ {j}(\theta_{k})\rvert}\,, \tag{5}\]

where the overbar denotes the mean with respective to \(\theta\). The hyperparameter \(\alpha\) is 0.9 following (Jin et al., 2021). This ensures that all components of the loss function contribute meaningfully to the optimization process, preventing any single term from dominating the training. The last term in Equation 4 (\(\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}\)) is the \(L_{2}\) regularization term (Girosi et al., 1995):

\[\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}=\lambda\sum_{j}w_{j}^{2}, \tag{6}\]

where \(\lambda\) is a regularization coefficient. This term is used to penalize large weights in a neural network and prevent discontinuity or overfitting (Wang et al., 2023).

Standard neural networks struggle to approximate high-frequency variations in time-dependent solutions, particularly for problems involving oscillatory dynamics such as tidal effects in river systems. To address this issue, Fourier feature embedding developed by Feng, Tan, and He (2023) is applied, which transforms the raw inputs into a higher-dimensional space before feeding them into the network (Figure 1(b)). Instead of directly using \(x\) and \(t\), we map them into a Fourier-transformed feature space \[\gamma(\mathbf{\nu})=\left[\begin{array}{c}\cos(2\pi\mathbf{B}\mathbf{\nu})\\ \sin(2\pi\mathbf{B}\mathbf{\nu})\end{array}\right], \tag{7}\]

where \(\gamma\) is the Fourier feature mapping function, \(\mathbf{\nu}=[x,t]^{T}\) is the input coordinates, and \(\mathbf{B}\) is the mapping matrix that defines the frequency components. In this study, we apply Fourier feature embedding only to the temporal coordinate \(t\). This ensures that the periodic nature of tidal wave propagation is encoded into the PINN framework. This technique significantly improved the accuracy of solutions near the river-ocean interface, where periodic forcing plays a dominant role.

### Finite-Difference-Based (FD) PINN

Despite their theoretical advantages, standard PINNs often suffer from a lack of training efficiency due to the intensive use of AD for calculating derivatives. This computational bottleneck limits their scalability, particularly in high-resolution simulations of hydrodynamics. To address these limitations, we introduce the Finite-difference-based PINN (FD-PINN) following Lim et al. (2022) and Jiang et al. (2023), which replaces AD with finite-difference numerical approximations of spatial and temporal derivatives (Figure 1(b)). Here, we use a second-order central difference method

\[\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}= \frac{f(x,t+\Delta t)-f(x,t-\Delta t)}{2\Delta t}, \tag{8}\] \[\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}= \frac{f(x+\Delta x,t)-f(x-\Delta x,t)}{2\Delta x}, \tag{9}\]

where \(\Delta x\) and \(\Delta t\) are the step sizes and \(f\) denotes the flow variables \(u\) and \(h\). Strict stability conditions are not required in FD-PINN as in traditional numerical solvers, since the governing equations in PINNs are applied as soft constraints. Stability-related issues can still arise. For example, the use of unnormalized flow variables with normalized space and time inputs can lead to unrealistically large PDE residuals and divergence during initial iterations.

Unlike standard PINNs that rely on AD and thus require traversing the full computational graph to compute PDE residuals, FD-PINN evaluates these residuals using explicit finite-difference formulas. This approach reduces memory usage and can accelerate training by avoiding the overhead of repeated AD operations, particularly in high-resolution settings. FD-PINN also enhances numerical stability by mitigating errors associated with vanishing or exploding gradients, particularly in high-gradient regions. However, both standard PINNs and FD-PINNs still rely on backpropagation for optimizing network parameters. Although the FD approach introduces mesh discretization, in contrast to the continuous nature of AD-based differentiation, it retains the core structure and training logics and parameters as standard PINNs. While finite-difference approaches have been previously introduced into PINN frameworks in other disciplines (e.g., for problems in electromagnetics or heat transport) (Jiang et al., 2023; Lim et al., 2022; Xiang et al., 2022), this study presents the first application of FD-PINN to the SVE for hydrodynamic modeling. By combining physics-informed learning with improved computational efficiency, FD-PINN provides a practical and scalable solution for river-coastal applications and integration into ESMs.

### Data-Driven Neural Networks

This study develops and evaluates a suite of data-driven neural networks for CF dynamics in the 1D river-ocean domain, including convolutional models, a hybrid CNN-LSTM model, a U-Net encoder-decoder architecture, and recurrent models (LSTM and GRU). Unlike PINNs, these models do not incorporate governing equations explicitly but instead rely on learning statistical relationships between boundary forcing and flood response from simulated hydrodynamic data.

The neural networks are trained to map input boundary conditions to output water depth distributions along the 1D domain. The selection of the model architectures is based on their effectiveness in capturing different hydrodynamic features relevant to CF modeling. CNN1D (referred as CNN throughout this study), a one-dimensionalconvolutional neural network, captures spatial correlations in flood wave propagation efficiently (Atashi et al., 2023). We evaluated three CNN-variants: CNN-FC, a fully connected CNN with a large dense output layer; CNN-Conv, a simplified fully convolutional model using 1D convolutions and a linear projection layer; and CNN-LSTM, a hybrid architecture combining convolutional feature extraction with temporal sequence modeling using LSTM layers. These variants represent different design trade-offs between complexity, efficiency, and temporal expressiveness. U-Net, a fully convolutional network with an encoder-decoder architecture and skip connections, is well-suited for capturing multi-scale flood dynamics (J. Li et al., 2024). LSTM and GRU, both RNN architectures, are selected due to their wide applications in streamflow predictions, where they effectively capture temporal dependencies in time series data (Le et al., 2021; Nevo et al., 2022). LSTM is designed to handle sequential dependencies and is effective in modeling long-term flood memory, while GRU, a variant of LSTM with fewer parameters, provides faster training while maintaining comparable predictive capability but may lose some expressiveness in capturing highly nonlinear flood dynamics. Each model is trained using a supervised learning approach, with the loss function minimizing the difference between predicted and simulated water depth. The trained models are evaluated based on prediction accuracy, computational efficiency, and generalization performance on an unseen extreme event.

#### 2.3.1 Data Set Generation

To ensure a robust and representative training data set, we develop a structured data processing and sampling approach that enhances the model's ability to generalize to extreme flood events (Figure 1(c)).

A key limitation of training ML models on the full streamflow and water level time series is the imbalance between frequent low-to-moderate floods and rare extreme floods, which can lead to biased predictions and poor generalization to extreme events (Kim et al., 2021). To address this, we developed a new method for training data generation (Figure 1(c)). This method generates synthetic training data using high-resolution hydrodynamic simulations, in which long-term observational records are used to inform the statistical characteristics of boundary forcing.

We first analyze historical flood records of river discharge at the upstream boundary and ocean water level at the downstream boundary to identify realistic and representative flood scenarios (Feng, Tan et al., 2022). For the selection of fluvial flood events from the gauged discharge time series, peak values are first identified using the peaks-over-threshold (POT) method (Lang et al., 1999). The threshold is determined through an automated selection process, ensuring that only independent flood peaks are retained (Zhang et al., 2021). To further ensure independence, a declustering technique is applied to eliminate closely spaced peaks. The selection of the storm surge event is performed by (a) extracting the storm surge component from the hourly total water level data and (b) using a peak detection algorithm to filter the extreme events (Feng, Tan et al., 2022). This method identifies periods where peak river discharge and extreme coastal storm surges occur within a given time window. These events are then ranked based on intensity of peak discharge and storm surge level, and a return period analysis is performed to ensure the inclusion of only significant flood scenarios. To focus on extreme conditions, we select events with a return period exceeding 5 years. This ensures that the training data set is not biased toward more frequent moderate floods.

Rather than using these historical events directly, we generate synthetic boundary conditions through Latin Hypercube Sampling (LHS) (Iman, 2008). Each sampled event represents a distinct realization of riverine and coastal forcing. The training data set thus captures a broad spectrum of possible CF conditions. We also introduce timing shifts to create additional variability while preserving physically realistic magnitudes. The synthetic combinations are then used as boundary forcing inputs to a high-resolution hydrodynamic model, which simulates water depth evolution over the event duration. A withheld extreme event is excluded from the training data set to assess model generalization on an unseen event during testing. This approach can produce a physically consistent, diverse, and statistically grounded data set for training data-driven models.

## 3 Model Application and Evaluation

### Study Domain and Hurricane Event

We selected the lower Delaware River reach as our study domain (Figure 3). Located along the U.S. Mid-Atlantic coast, the Delaware River supplies drinking water to approximately 6% of the U.S. population. The lower reach is characterized by a relatively flat topography and is influenced by the upstream propagation of tidal forces--primarily driven by \(M_{2}\) and S\({}_{2}\) semiditural constituents--as well as storm surges from Delaware Bay and upstream discharge from the Delaware River. This region was chosen not only for its complex hydrodynamics but also because we have previously configured a global Earth System Model (ESM) using the Energy Exascale Earth System model (E3SM) with a tailored mesh design for both river and ocean models (Figure S1 in Supporting Information S1) (Feng et al., 2024). The river model mesh is refined to 3 km resolution within the Mid-Atlantic watersheds. The ocean model features a high-resolution 250 m mesh along the U.S. East Coast, transitioning to 1 km resolution elsewhere. Despite these refinements, a substantial resolution mismatch remains between the river and ocean model components. Further refining the river mesh is not conceptually appropriate given the model's inherent assumption (Li et al., 2013). Using the high-resolution ocean mesh for the entire interface region is also computationally inefficient. For example, a 10-day Hurricane Irene simulation takes approximately 10 min on 400 CPUs for the river model, whereas the 2D barotropic ocean component requires over 5 hr. This configuration highlights a key limitation in current ESMs: even with refinement, coarse-resolution river models struggle to capture localized fine-scale hydrodynamic processes at the river-ocean interface. The 1D river channel test domain in this study is extracted from the E3SM's river model mesh. Although E3SM is not directly used in the simulations here, its configuration provides the basis for higher-resolution modeling of river-ocean interactions. This study serves as an intermediate step toward identifying ML models suitable for future integration into the same E3SM configurations.

Hurricane Irene, which made landfall on the U.S. East Coast on 27 August 2011, was selected as a representative CF event due to its significant hydrometeorological impacts on the Delaware River region. This event was characterized by extreme rainfall within Delaware River Basin and peak storm surge of \(>\)1.8 m in Delaware Bay (Avila & Cangialosi, 2011). The compound nature of Irene makes it an ideal case for analyzing the ML models' performance in simulating the interplay between riverine and coastal flooding mechanisms in a CF scenario. To the best of our knowledge, it is the only major hurricane-induced CF event recorded in this specific domain.

