### Numerical Model Setup and Data Collection

In the extracted 1D river-ocean domain, a high-resolution hydrodynamic model was configured using the TELEMAC-MASCARET modeling system (Feng et al., 2025; Hervouet, 2000) to provide both training and reference data for the ML models. Although the global E3SM also encompasses this region (Feng et al., 2024), its 10-day Hurricane Irene simulation cannot sample the full spectrum of CF scenarios needed for robust model training. TELEMAC is selected for its ability to solve the full dynamic SVE and its efficiency to run on high-performance computing (HPC) systems. The latter is important for generating the large ensemble of simulations needed to train and evaluate data-driven models. The model was configured with a spatial resolution of 200 m in both the along-channel and cross-channel directions, where the river topology data are from Feng et al. (2024) (Figure 3; Figure S2 in Supporting Information S1). Although the original setup was two-dimensional (2D), careful evaluation confirmed that variations in the cross-channel direction were negligible, which justifies the reduction to a 1D framework while preserving the essential flood dynamics.

To perform TELEMAC simulations over various historical events and generate the ensemble training data sets, the upstream and downstream boundary conditions were obtained from the closest NOAA and USGS gauge stations, respectively (Figures 3; Figures 4a and 4b). The model was run for 10 days, and the output was archived at 1-hr intervals. The simulation was first spun up for 1 month, after which a restart file was saved. This restart condition was then used to run an ensemble of event-scale simulations, generating a diverse and physically consistent training data set for the data-driven models.

### Machine Learning Model Setup

In the PINN and FD-PINN training, the synthetic remote sensing data at the initial and last time steps, assuming a 10-day revisiting time, and synthetic in situ times-series observations over the simulation period are from the TELEMAC simulation. The in situ observation sites are imposed at three evenly distributed locations along the channel (Figure 2a). While the in situ measurements are generally considered highly accurate (Turnipseed and Sauer, 2010), remote sensing data, even when refined to resolutions on the order of 10 m, such as SWOT data (Domeneghetti et al., 2018; Geiriana et al., 2024), may introduce greater uncertainties. To evaluate the robustness of the PINN framework to observational noise, we imposed noise levels of 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, 5%, and 10% on the synthetic remote sensing data. For both PINN and FD-PINN, the collocation points where the PDE residuals are evaluated were uniformly distributed with a spatial resolution of 400 m and a temporal resolution of 1 hr, which ensures sufficient resolution for model accuracy while avoiding excessive computational overhead during training. The architecture of PINN and FD-PINN consists of a fully connected feed-forward DNN with five hidden layers, each containing 64 neurons. We employed the widely used initialization scheme of Xavier scheme (Glorot and Bengio, 2010). This configuration follows the design used in Cases 5 and 6 of Feng, Tan, and He (2023), which were trained with a comparable amount of data. The models are trained for 80,000 iterations,

